## What doesn't belong here

- Files of other kinds — those belong in folders dedicated to their kind
- Personal drafts, scratch work, or unfinished sketches — keep those out of version control or in a `_drafts/` subfolder
- Generated artifacts — put outputs in a dedicated build directory

## How to add a {{file_kind}}

1. Open [TEMPLATE.md](TEMPLATE.md) and read it through before drafting. The shape matters more than the rule list — match the template's structure rather than improvising.
2. Copy `TEMPLATE.md` to a new file named according to the naming convention below.
3. Fill in the frontmatter honestly. Frontmatter is what lets readers self-select; sloppy frontmatter weakens the whole folder.
4. Keep it focused. If a {{file_kind}} starts trying to cover too much, it probably wants to split.

## Naming

- Files: `{{naming_convention}}`
- Sequence numbers (if used) are stable once published. Reordering happens via frontmatter, not by renumbering files.

## Conventions

- Every {{file_kind}} should have an attributed author and date in its frontmatter. Provenance matters.
- Prefer real, copyable content over abstract description. Concrete examples produce better downstream results than rules in prose.
- Link outward to existing material rather than re-explaining it.

## A note on how this file loads

This folder-level `AGENTS.md` follows the nested-instruction pattern (see Decision 014 in the plugin that scaffolded it, and Principle 7 in `design-principles.md`). Loading timing differs by host:

- **Claude Code** lazy-loads this file — it enters context only when the agent reads a file inside this folder. If you're authoring a new {{file_kind}} from outside `{{folder_path}}/`, the rules here may not be in context yet. Reference [TEMPLATE.md](TEMPLATE.md) explicitly in your prompt to be safe.
- **OpenAI Codex** loads this file eagerly when the working directory is inside `{{folder_path}}/`, alongside the root `AGENTS.md`.

Both behaviours are valid and documented; awareness of which one applies improves outcomes.

